
  Next fork. Right now a validation failure just calls [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m — the user sees nothing. Whether the shared layer
  owns error [3mdisplay[0m or only produces error [3mdata[0m decides its surface area: a data-only validator is easy to test and
  easy to reuse in Node, but every form re-writes the same DOM code for showing messages; a layer that also renders
  errors kills that duplication but ties the module to a specific markup convention, and changing that convention later
  means touching every form's HTML. Data-only is cheap to extend into rendering later; rendering is expensive to back
  out of.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How much should the shared layer own? → Validate + render errors (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — the shared layer validates and renders. That makes the markup convention part of the contract, so I'll pin it
  explicitly in the spec.

  One more constraint question. The browser app loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag, and [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS — two
  incompatible conventions, and a shared validation file has to pick one. ES modules mean [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m and
  real [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m, which is also what makes the module testable in Node later; the cost is that [38;5;153mfile://[39m opening of
  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m stops working (modules need an HTTP server) and it's a change to existing HTML. A second plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag
  that defines a global keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working and changes nothing, but leaves you with an implicit load-order
  dependency and a global that's awkward to test. A bundler solves both and adds a toolchain this repo currently doesn't
  have.
